 HTML 4.0//EN\" \"http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40/strict.dtd\">\n<html><head><meta name=\"qrichtext\" content=\"1\" /><meta charset=\"utf-8\" /><style type=\"text/css\">\np, li { white-space: pre-wrap; }\nhr { height: 1px; border-width: 0; }\nli.unchecked::marker { content: \"\\2610\"; }\nli.checked::marker { content: \"\\2612\"; }\n</style></head><body style=\" font-family:'Ubuntu'; font-size:13px; font-weight:400; font-style:normal;\">\n<p style=\" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;\"><span style=\" font-size:12px;\">This example shows how to use tracking-zones, a simple </span></p>\n<p style=\" margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;\"><span style=\" font-size:12px;\">object to allow triggering actions in 2D or 3D according to<br />zones defined in a graphical environment.<br /><br />The sources can understand most tracking data sources<br />than score can emit: <br />- Tracked points through Pose Detector. Right now the first<br />outlet is connected ; if using a model / configuration that supports <br />multi-pose detection then use the third outlet to have each pose independent.<br />- Raw series of keypoints, e.g. [x,y,x,y,x,y] or [x,y,z,x,y,z,x,y,z] - the second outlet <br />of PoseDetector or any raw &quot;unformatted&quot; keypoint data without tracking <br />information<br />- Input from the various tracking devices and protocols supported: TUIO, OptiTrack<br />etc.<br />- Input from the Point Tracker 2D / 3D<br />etc.</span></p></body></html>"},"Init":{"String":"(Double-click to edit)"},"Domain":{}},{"uuid":"fb27e4cb-ea7f-41e2-ad92-2354498c1b6b","ObjectName":"Inlet","id":1,"Hidden":true,"Custom":"Auto-reflow","Exposed":"auto-reflow","Value":{"Bool":false},"Init":{"Bool":false},"Domain":{"Bool":null}}],"Outlets":[]},{"uuid":"3f4a41f2-fa39-420f-ab0f-0af6b8409edb","ObjectName":"Display","id":6,"Metadata":{"ScriptingName"